WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:127826

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:04.971
So first a little bit of setup for our modern OCR stack.

2
00:00:05.372 --> 00:00:09.376
We're going to import PIL for images, OpenCV and numpy

3
00:00:09.576 --> 00:00:12.178
for image processing, matplotlib for plotting,

4
00:00:12.579 --> 00:00:15.582
and of course PaddleOCR from PaddleOCR.

5
00:00:16.149 --> 00:00:17.951
This cell is importing the API

6
00:00:17.951 --> 00:00:20.053
key that we'll need for the agent,

7
00:00:20.253 --> 00:00:22.255
very similar to the previous lab.

8
00:00:22.856 --> 00:00:25.225
Having completed our setup, let's get into it.

9
00:00:25.425 --> 00:00:28.328
All right, so here we create a PaddleOCR object

10
00:00:28.528 --> 00:00:31.031
with the language specified as English.

11
00:00:31.364 --> 00:00:34.868
And notice that the two models from the architecture diagram

12
00:00:35.035 --> 00:00:37.537
from the slides are represented here.

13
00:00:37.837 --> 00:00:42.142
So here's that _DET text detector model,

14
00:00:42.342 --> 00:00:45.845
and here's the _REC recognition model.

15
00:00:47.213 --> 00:00:50.116
Now, you won't actually call these models sequentially.

16
00:00:50.216 --> 00:00:53.119
PaddleOCR is going to handle all of the pre-processing

17
00:00:53.219 --> 00:00:56.222
and calling these models all as one pipeline.

18
00:00:56.723 --> 00:00:59.692
This receipt image will look familiar from lab one.

19
00:01:00.260 --> 00:01:02.262
This cell runs the OCR.

20
00:01:02.862 --> 00:01:05.765
The result will be a list where each item

21
00:01:05.865 --> 00:01:07.867
corresponds to a processed page.

22
00:01:08.268 --> 00:01:10.270
So for a single page image,

23
00:01:10.270 --> 00:01:12.572
it's only going to contain one dictionary.

24
00:01:13.339 --> 00:01:15.508
So this cell is going to print

25
00:01:15.508 --> 00:01:17.644
some of the contents of result zero.

26
00:01:18.344 --> 00:01:20.947
Specifically the text that was recognized,

27
00:01:21.948 --> 00:01:24.451
the confidence score associated with that

28
00:01:25.318 --> 00:01:27.520
and the bounding box coordinates.

29
00:01:28.121 --> 00:01:31.024
And as we scroll down, we'll see all of the text

30
00:01:31.024 --> 00:01:33.526
that was recognized across the entire receipt.

31
00:01:34.828 --> 00:01:36.529
All right, before we execute this

32
00:01:36.529 --> 00:01:38.531
cell, let me explain what to expect.

33
00:01:39.332 --> 00:01:42.202
Recall from the architecture diagram that the pipeline

34
00:01:42.502 --> 00:01:45.505
also pre-processes the image where necessary.

35
00:01:45.905 --> 00:01:48.808
And it can correct rotational issues

36
00:01:48.808 --> 00:01:51.010
or deskew or unwarp the image.

37
00:01:51.544 --> 00:01:55.748
So what we're going to see now is actually
the pre-processed image

38
00:01:55.748 --> 00:01:59.252
with the bounding box and the text recognition overlaid.

39
00:01:59.752 --> 00:02:02.055
So let's go ahead and request the image.

40
00:02:03.156 --> 00:02:06.659
and then execute another cell where we can view it

41
00:02:07.060 --> 00:02:09.062
with all of those items overlaid.

42
00:02:09.963 --> 00:02:12.966
Now, if you compare this image carefully to the original,

43
00:02:13.366 --> 00:02:16.169
you'll actually see that some of the background is removed

44
00:02:16.469 --> 00:02:18.771
and there is a slight rotation clockwise

45
00:02:18.972 --> 00:02:21.374
to make it a little bit more horizontal.

46
00:02:22.609 --> 00:02:26.312
And then the code draws the bounding boxes on the processed image

47
00:02:26.412 --> 00:02:28.715
and places the recognized text on the top.

48
00:02:29.415 --> 00:02:32.018
So why repeat an example such as this one?

49
00:02:32.285 --> 00:02:34.787
Well, notice the addition of the bounding boxes.

50
00:02:34.787 --> 00:02:37.090
We now have localization information

51
00:02:37.090 --> 00:02:39.092
about where in the receipt

52
00:02:39.092 --> 00:02:41.794
a particular text field is found.

53
00:02:42.295 --> 00:02:45.064
And also notice that we are getting the value correct.

54
00:02:45.064 --> 00:02:48.067
Previously this 795 was incorrect.

55
00:02:48.635 --> 00:02:52.138
All right, so now we're going to turn PaddleOCR into a tool

56
00:02:52.138 --> 00:02:54.240
that can be used with an agent.

57
00:02:55.141 --> 00:02:57.443
This will be very similar to lab one.

58
00:02:58.144 --> 00:03:01.548
So this long function, we're setting this up as a tool,

59
00:03:02.115 --> 00:03:05.919
and here the result is going to be coming from the PaddleOCR,

60
00:03:06.419 --> 00:03:08.221
and much of the rest is the same.

61
00:03:08.521 --> 00:03:11.024
We're going to be printing out the text

62
00:03:11.024 --> 00:03:13.726
and the boxes and the confidence scores

63
00:03:13.726 --> 00:03:16.029
as we saw in the prior example.

64
00:03:17.030 --> 00:03:19.032
All right, having defined that function,

65
00:03:19.032 --> 00:03:22.335
we'll now specify that that function is the tool

66
00:03:22.335 --> 00:03:25.038
that will be available to the agent.

67
00:03:25.738 --> 00:03:27.040
The rest of this is going

68
00:03:27.040 --> 00:03:28.541
to be the same as lesson one,

69
00:03:28.741 --> 00:03:32.345
and note that we're using gpt-5-mini as our LLM.

70
00:03:34.047 --> 00:03:36.316
This task should look familiar as well.

71
00:03:36.416 --> 00:03:38.218
We're going to use the receipt

72
00:03:38.218 --> 00:03:40.320
and evaluate that the total is correct.

73
00:03:40.787 --> 00:03:42.889
So, as I scroll down here,

74
00:03:42.889 --> 00:03:46.292
of course we see the PaddleOCR output here in the turquoise,

75
00:03:46.292 --> 00:03:48.995
the green output is coming from the LLM.

76
00:03:49.596 --> 00:03:52.599
The task again was to perform some basic addition,

77
00:03:52.599 --> 00:03:55.001
and because the inputs are correct,

78
00:03:55.001 --> 00:03:58.605
then the addition has a much greater chance of being correct,

79
00:03:58.605 --> 00:04:00.573
and we do get the correct total.

80
00:04:01.241 --> 00:04:04.043
So this example really shows how a combination

81
00:04:04.143 --> 00:04:06.746
of PaddleOCR and an LLM

82
00:04:06.846 --> 00:04:09.349
can handle a real world receipt.

83
00:04:10.149 --> 00:04:13.152
Remember the Table exercise and the student Handwriting,

84
00:04:13.253 --> 00:04:15.255
we're going to do those two next.

85
00:04:15.521 --> 00:04:17.624
We're going to define another helper function.

86
00:04:17.824 --> 00:04:19.826
There's actually nothing new to see here.

87
00:04:20.026 --> 00:04:23.029
So the OCR result is still coming from PaddleOCR.

88
00:04:23.329 --> 00:04:25.732
We've still got some printed output.

89
00:04:26.332 --> 00:04:30.136
We're still working with the post-processed image,

90
00:04:30.536 --> 00:04:31.904
and then we're going to be

91
00:04:31.904 --> 00:04:33.539
doing some annotations on top of that

92
00:04:33.539 --> 00:04:35.541
using the bounding box coordinates.

93
00:04:35.842 --> 00:04:38.544
But now it's wrapped into one function called run_ocr,

94
00:04:38.845 --> 00:04:42.148
and you'll see this one repeatedly through the rest of the lesson.

95
00:04:42.482 --> 00:04:45.885
All right, so let's apply all of that to the table.

96
00:04:46.886 --> 00:04:48.788
This is a reminder of what it looks like.

97
00:04:49.689 --> 00:04:54.294
And our second chunk of output is the PaddleOCR printed information.

98
00:04:54.494 --> 00:04:58.598
Again, we've got the recognized text, confidence score, and bounding boxes.

99
00:04:59.098 --> 00:05:03.970
Scrolling on past that, we have the actual overlaid bounding boxes

100
00:05:04.070 --> 00:05:06.372
with the recognized text in blue.

101
00:05:07.273 --> 00:05:09.876
So, as you visually inspect this,

102
00:05:10.076 --> 00:05:12.879
you may detect one or more OCR errors.

103
00:05:13.579 --> 00:05:16.883
And the first one that I see actually continues

104
00:05:16.883 --> 00:05:18.985
with the treatment of the exponential notation.

105
00:05:19.585 --> 00:05:21.387
We had problems with this previously.

106
00:05:21.754 --> 00:05:24.157
The 10th to the 20th over here

107
00:05:24.457 --> 00:05:26.859
is recognized as 1020,

108
00:05:27.360 --> 00:05:30.463
and that's like a billion billions difference from the real answer.

109
00:05:31.064 --> 00:05:33.132
So it also seems like most of

110
00:05:33.132 --> 00:05:35.168
the exponents are recognized in this way.

111
00:05:35.902 --> 00:05:38.504
But let's go ahead and power forward.

112
00:05:39.205 --> 00:05:43.209
We'll give the agent the task of again extracting the flops

113
00:05:43.509 --> 00:05:48.509
from the English to German, noted as EN-DE column in the table.

114
00:05:50.883 --> 00:05:53.086
All right, let's scroll through this output.

115
00:05:53.486 --> 00:05:56.189
Again, the turquoise is coming from PaddleOCR.

116
00:05:57.790 --> 00:05:59.892
The green is coming from the LLM.

117
00:06:00.393 --> 00:06:03.196
And then in our function we also asked for

118
00:06:03.196 --> 00:06:05.398
this printed result down at the bottom.

119
00:06:06.899 --> 00:06:09.902
So you can do the visual inspection for yourself

120
00:06:09.902 --> 00:06:13.206
or you can trust me that this is actually fully correct.

121
00:06:13.573 --> 00:06:16.576
And let me draw your attention to two aspects

122
00:06:16.676 --> 00:06:19.379
that were incorrect in the previous run.

123
00:06:20.546 --> 00:06:22.548
So this ByteNet

124
00:06:22.648 --> 00:06:24.951
and this Deep-Att

125
00:06:25.051 --> 00:06:27.754
are correctly noted as not found,

126
00:06:27.854 --> 00:06:31.057
because they are blank areas in the original table.

127
00:06:31.457 --> 00:06:35.261
And fortunately, our scientific notation has been corrected.

128
00:06:35.661 --> 00:06:38.297
Instead of 1020, this is now

129
00:06:38.297 --> 00:06:41.367
reported as 10 to the 20th power.

130
00:06:42.268 --> 00:06:45.071
And how is that possible? This actually

131
00:06:45.071 --> 00:06:47.907
nicely demonstrates the reasoning power of LLMs

132
00:06:47.907 --> 00:06:50.610
because for something like FLOPs,

133
00:06:50.610 --> 00:06:53.913
which stands for the floating point operations,

134
00:06:53.913 --> 00:06:57.617
it simply doesn't make sense for the result to be 10 20.

135
00:06:57.617 --> 00:07:00.286
It needs to be a much larger number.

136
00:07:00.286 --> 00:07:03.289
So the agent has been able to apply reasoning

137
00:07:03.289 --> 00:07:06.292
to the OCR output and remedy that situation.

138
00:07:07.493 --> 00:07:10.496
Let's see if we can also remedy the issue

139
00:07:10.596 --> 00:07:13.199
with the student handwritten grammar exercise.

140
00:07:13.599 --> 00:07:15.802
Here's a reminder of what it looks like.

141
00:07:17.103 --> 00:07:19.505
Let's take a look at the output

142
00:07:19.605 --> 00:07:21.908
and notice at the top here that the name is John Smith

143
00:07:22.008 --> 00:07:24.811
and it seems pretty legible to me.

144
00:07:25.478 --> 00:07:29.782
But the student name here is detected as Myar

145
00:07:30.183 --> 00:07:32.485
and I'm not even going to try to say that.

146
00:07:32.785 --> 00:07:34.787
But it's clearly not John Smith.

147
00:07:35.321 --> 00:07:39.091
Let's scroll down to the overlaid bounding boxes

148
00:07:39.492 --> 00:07:41.794
and call attention to a few other things.

149
00:07:42.795 --> 00:07:46.098
So question number one is very clearly I am,

150
00:07:46.499 --> 00:07:50.503
but in the text recognition, I think we're getting I underscore

151
00:07:50.803 --> 00:07:52.872
and then this looks like a u

152
00:07:52.872 --> 00:07:54.907
a n to me. So that's not

153
00:07:55.107 --> 00:07:58.978
good. Number two, you know, we've got an extra underscore in here.

154
00:07:58.978 --> 00:08:01.180
We could probably clean that up in post-processing,

155
00:08:01.180 --> 00:08:02.815
but it would certainly be nice

156
00:08:02.815 --> 00:08:04.684
if it was accurate from the beginning.

157
00:08:05.184 --> 00:08:07.353
And for item number nine, we

158
00:08:07.353 --> 00:08:09.889
are able to recognize this person's handwriting

159
00:08:09.889 --> 00:08:13.993
as is, which is an improvement over the Tesseract performance.

160
00:08:14.894 --> 00:08:17.096
So now let's give the agent the task

161
00:08:17.096 --> 00:08:20.366
of extracting the student responses as JSON

162
00:08:20.766 --> 00:08:23.169
and notice that the task instructs

163
00:08:23.569 --> 00:08:26.072
do not correct any grammatical errors.

164
00:08:26.739 --> 00:08:30.543
All right, let's scroll down to the responses

165
00:08:31.244 --> 00:08:35.548
and we'll actually just skip right ahead to the LLM response here.

166
00:08:36.749 --> 00:08:40.553
So, question one here is somewhat nonsensical.

167
00:08:41.254 --> 00:08:44.557
And we've already noticed that the name is completely messed up.

168
00:08:44.957 --> 00:08:46.959
But there's some good news here too.

169
00:08:47.260 --> 00:08:49.562
So the JSON formatting is correct.

170
00:08:49.762 --> 00:08:54.233
That's some good news and the incorrect grammatical answer

171
00:08:55.034 --> 00:08:57.803
has stayed true to the student's original response.

172
00:08:58.204 --> 00:09:00.439
Again, this was a grammar worksheet,

173
00:09:00.439 --> 00:09:03.976
and so we don't want the LLM overwriting the student's response.

174
00:09:04.977 --> 00:09:07.513
So to wrap up this section,

175
00:09:07.513 --> 00:09:10.483
we've repeated three exercises from lesson one.

176
00:09:11.284 --> 00:09:14.654
And recall from the PyTesseract architecture diagram

177
00:09:15.254 --> 00:09:18.958
that there is a detection stage and a recognition stage.

178
00:09:19.325 --> 00:09:23.329
So the detection is what's giving us the bounding boxes,

179
00:09:23.329 --> 00:09:28.134
and that is clearly helping with the understanding of the overall document.

180
00:09:28.134 --> 00:09:31.137
And the recognition here is also better.

181
00:09:31.137 --> 00:09:34.340
There are fewer actual character-level mistakes

182
00:09:34.340 --> 00:09:37.343
in terms of transcribing or recognizing the text.

183
00:09:37.343 --> 00:09:41.847
So definitely performing better on these identical examples.

184
00:09:42.381 --> 00:09:45.184
And now we'll move on to some more difficult examples

185
00:09:45.885 --> 00:09:47.887
to expose some weaknesses.

186
00:09:48.387 --> 00:09:51.591
So in this section, we're going to look at three new examples

187
00:09:51.591 --> 00:09:55.895
and we have specifically selected them to expose some weaknesses.

188
00:09:56.195 --> 00:09:58.397
But these are things that you need to be aware of

189
00:09:58.397 --> 00:10:00.199
if you're building with PaddleOCR.

190
00:10:00.600 --> 00:10:02.702
So this is a new example.

191
00:10:02.702 --> 00:10:04.604
It's called report.png

192
00:10:04.604 --> 00:10:07.506
and it appears to be an interior page

193
00:10:07.506 --> 00:10:10.209
from a report about the US economy.

194
00:10:10.843 --> 00:10:12.845
At the top there's a table,

195
00:10:12.845 --> 00:10:15.147
the center has some basic text,

196
00:10:15.648 --> 00:10:17.850
and at the bottom there's a line chart

197
00:10:17.850 --> 00:10:20.152
with a caption to the left.

198
00:10:20.853 --> 00:10:23.456
Let's take a look at how PaddleOCR

199
00:10:23.456 --> 00:10:25.458
has extracted this document.

200
00:10:26.459 --> 00:10:30.463
So at first glance, the table output actually looks quite good

201
00:10:30.630 --> 00:10:33.132
and the text is fairly straightforward.

202
00:10:33.299 --> 00:10:35.501
But let's take a look at this line chart.

203
00:10:36.502 --> 00:10:38.804
So there's no box around the whole thing.

204
00:10:39.105 --> 00:10:41.240
So that's my first clue that it's

205
00:10:41.240 --> 00:10:43.376
not being recognized as a single unit.

206
00:10:43.743 --> 00:10:45.645
But there are boxes around some

207
00:10:45.645 --> 00:10:47.847
of the X and Y axis labels,

208
00:10:48.147 --> 00:10:52.351
such as this 0, -2, -4, -6.

209
00:10:52.752 --> 00:10:55.655
And these are now completely out of context,

210
00:10:55.955 --> 00:10:58.658
which we can see if we scroll back up.

211
00:10:59.959 --> 00:11:02.461
So indeed, here's that zero,

212
00:11:02.461 --> 00:11:05.765
completely disconnected from this -2,

213
00:11:05.765 --> 00:11:09.769
separated by some other content and then -4.

214
00:11:09.769 --> 00:11:13.773
So there's really no way of understanding that these were Y-axis labels

215
00:11:13.773 --> 00:11:17.777
or that they actually belonged to a chart which was ignored completely.

216
00:11:18.244 --> 00:11:20.546
So we've exposed one weakness.

217
00:11:21.547 --> 00:11:26.252
All right, let's take a look at article.jpg. What does this contain?

218
00:11:26.752 --> 00:11:30.056
This looks like it's the front page of an academic article

219
00:11:30.056 --> 00:11:32.058
about teeth.

220
00:11:32.658 --> 00:11:35.261
Okay, so what do we notice about this article?

221
00:11:35.761 --> 00:11:38.431
So there are multiple columns of text here.

222
00:11:38.431 --> 00:11:41.734
So at the top of the article, there's kind of two columns.

223
00:11:41.734 --> 00:11:44.437
We've got the abstract next to this call out.

224
00:11:44.670 --> 00:11:46.539
And then the body of course

225
00:11:46.539 --> 00:11:48.741
is presented as three columns of text,

226
00:11:48.841 --> 00:11:51.444
which is kind of interrupted by this table.

227
00:11:51.844 --> 00:11:55.548
So, let's scroll down to view the bounding box output

228
00:11:55.648 --> 00:11:59.251
and see how well it does with this multi-column document.

229
00:12:00.286 --> 00:12:02.588
All right, there's a lot of boxes here,

230
00:12:02.888 --> 00:12:05.091
but I'm just going to draw your attention

231
00:12:05.091 --> 00:12:07.093
to the first and second column.

232
00:12:07.593 --> 00:12:09.895
And of course, the reading order here,

233
00:12:09.895 --> 00:12:12.898
you would read it in most of the westernised countries

234
00:12:12.898 --> 00:12:16.602
that undertake oral health surveys, right?

235
00:12:16.869 --> 00:12:20.272
So we would be reading all the way down the left-hand column.

236
00:12:20.673 --> 00:12:23.476
But in the OCR, we're actually going to see

237
00:12:23.776 --> 00:12:26.979
reading straight across if you're following my mouse.

238
00:12:27.580 --> 00:12:30.583
So it would be in most of the westernized countries

239
00:12:30.883 --> 00:12:33.786
that system based on some of the interview,

240
00:12:34.286 --> 00:12:36.589
which of course makes absolutely no sense

241
00:12:36.889 --> 00:12:39.291
and manages to garble the entire article

242
00:12:39.592 --> 00:12:41.894
if you continue that way for, you know,

243
00:12:41.894 --> 00:12:43.896
another 10, 15 pages.

244
00:12:45.297 --> 00:12:48.634
So what we've learned about PaddleOCR is that it is not able

245
00:12:48.701 --> 00:12:52.238
to handle these multi-column layouts

246
00:12:52.304 --> 00:12:54.907
and runs the risk of garbling your text

247
00:12:55.007 --> 00:12:58.210
if this is a layout that's part of your document.

248
00:12:58.978 --> 00:13:02.481
So we've surfaced these weaknesses around layout,

249
00:13:02.681 --> 00:13:07.153
and we're starting to realize that layout-aware text detection

250
00:13:07.153 --> 00:13:12.057
is really going to be at the cornerstone of accurate real-world OCR

251
00:13:12.057 --> 00:13:15.161
because of course, layouts like this are very common.

252
00:13:15.861 --> 00:13:19.165
But the PaddleOCR model doesn't have vision.

253
00:13:19.465 --> 00:13:21.867
We actually are going to need some sort of vision model

254
00:13:22.168 --> 00:13:23.869
for more complex documents.

255
00:13:24.570 --> 00:13:28.174
So good news, PaddleOCR actually does have its own version

256
00:13:28.274 --> 00:13:30.976
of Layout Detection. And as we mentioned

257
00:13:30.976 --> 00:13:34.480
before, it has been going through active development

258
00:13:34.880 --> 00:13:37.483
and we haven't been using the Layout Detection previously.

259
00:13:37.983 --> 00:13:40.986
So here's our chance to import that and start to use it

260
00:13:40.986 --> 00:13:45.791
in conjunction with the text detection and recognition.

261
00:13:46.292 --> 00:13:50.095
So here we will initialize that LayoutDetection model.

262
00:13:51.096 --> 00:13:54.099
So we'll define a new function called process_document

263
00:13:54.800 --> 00:13:58.504
and we'll send our image to this layout_engine.

264
00:13:59.338 --> 00:14:03.142
And as part of the response, we'll get back the label,

265
00:14:03.342 --> 00:14:06.145
the score, and the bounding boxes.

266
00:14:06.812 --> 00:14:10.115
So the score and the bounding boxes we've seen before,

267
00:14:10.216 --> 00:14:13.118
but now we're going to get a label for that region.

268
00:14:14.119 --> 00:14:16.422
And that'll make more sense once you see it.

269
00:14:17.823 --> 00:14:20.326
All right, let's apply process_document

270
00:14:20.426 --> 00:14:24.129
to that interior page from the report on the US economy.

271
00:14:24.563 --> 00:14:27.166
Hopefully now the meaning of label is more clear.

272
00:14:27.466 --> 00:14:30.369
We're getting back things such as text, chart,

273
00:14:30.569 --> 00:14:33.772
paragraph_title, or number, or even footer.

274
00:14:34.173 --> 00:14:37.476
So this is identifying the different regions on the document

275
00:14:37.476 --> 00:14:39.678
and labeling them for what they are.

276
00:14:41.480 --> 00:14:43.148
And the purpose of this long

277
00:14:43.148 --> 00:14:45.117
function is just to aid in visualization.

278
00:14:46.819 --> 00:14:49.922
So let's look again at that economic report,

279
00:14:49.922 --> 00:14:52.391
but now with the layout detection overlaid.

280
00:14:53.225 --> 00:14:55.794
Let's take a look at things here.

281
00:14:56.161 --> 00:14:57.796
I'm seeing several text blocks.

282
00:14:57.997 --> 00:14:59.698
I'm seeing a paragraph title.

283
00:14:59.899 --> 00:15:02.034
I'm seeing a table up here,

284
00:15:02.034 --> 00:15:05.004
and very importantly, I'm seeing a chart

285
00:15:05.004 --> 00:15:07.706
and an entire bounding box around that chart.

286
00:15:08.007 --> 00:15:11.110
And then also small details such as number and footer.

287
00:15:11.477 --> 00:15:14.280
But the layout model really now has identified

288
00:15:14.380 --> 00:15:17.249
the major regions of this document correctly.

289
00:15:18.250 --> 00:15:21.954
And now you'll do the same thing with the article about teeth.

290
00:15:23.055 --> 00:15:25.257
So as I'm visually taking this in,

291
00:15:25.457 --> 00:15:27.660
I'm seeing labels that I've seen before,

292
00:15:27.860 --> 00:15:30.362
such as text and paragraph_title.

293
00:15:30.663 --> 00:15:34.266
I'm seeing a few new ones, such as doc_title and abstract.

294
00:15:34.466 --> 00:15:37.469
I'm seeing high confidence scores associated with those.

295
00:15:38.003 --> 00:15:41.807
And then as I scroll down, seeing some footnote

296
00:15:42.007 --> 00:15:44.510
and footer and also table.

297
00:15:45.010 --> 00:15:48.814
But I will note that the table to me is one table,

298
00:15:49.014 --> 00:15:53.485
and it's being recognized here as two tables with slightly lower confidence.

299
00:15:53.919 --> 00:15:55.721
But all in all, the layout

300
00:15:55.721 --> 00:15:57.823
model is now really going to help

301
00:15:57.923 --> 00:15:59.725
keep this text together.

302
00:16:00.125 --> 00:16:02.127
So we no longer have the problem

303
00:16:02.428 --> 00:16:05.230
of continuing from the word that to the word system

304
00:16:05.764 --> 00:16:08.767
because this entire text block is going to indicate

305
00:16:08.767 --> 00:16:11.870
that the word after that is undertake.

306
00:16:12.638 --> 00:16:13.839
So let's see how far we

307
00:16:13.839 --> 00:16:15.240
can push this with one more example.

308
00:16:16.575 --> 00:16:18.777
This one is a bank statement,

309
00:16:18.777 --> 00:16:21.680
and typically when dealing with bank statements,

310
00:16:21.680 --> 00:16:23.682
the ultimate downstream use case

311
00:16:23.682 --> 00:16:25.351
is to extract a certain number

312
00:16:25.351 --> 00:16:27.286
of key value pairs out of it.

313
00:16:27.953 --> 00:16:31.890
And I'm actually seeing the same challenge with the table.

314
00:16:32.291 --> 00:16:35.561
In this case, this is all being detected as one large table,

315
00:16:35.961 --> 00:16:38.464
but for me, you know, with my human vision

316
00:16:38.764 --> 00:16:41.867
and human experience looking at bank statements,

317
00:16:42.067 --> 00:16:44.970
can actually tell that the headers should have been right here.

318
00:16:45.070 --> 00:16:48.574
Date, Description, Category, Amount, and Balance.

319
00:16:49.108 --> 00:16:52.611
That's actually the table break and the table headers.

320
00:16:52.911 --> 00:16:56.315
Everything up above could be interpreted as a table,

321
00:16:56.615 --> 00:16:59.618
but it should definitely be a separate table from the one below.

322
00:16:59.985 --> 00:17:02.788
So there's definitely still some weaknesses here.

323
00:17:03.489 --> 00:17:05.524
Oddly in this example, the small

324
00:17:05.524 --> 00:17:07.893
text at the bottom is entirely ignored,

325
00:17:08.494 --> 00:17:10.696
and sometimes that's where a legal footnote

326
00:17:10.796 --> 00:17:13.098
or other important information might appear.

327
00:17:13.465 --> 00:17:16.635
All right, so that brings us to the end of lab two.

328
00:17:16.935 --> 00:17:20.239
And let me recap a few of the main takeaways.

329
00:17:20.439 --> 00:17:24.643
So PaddleOCR is from the Deep Learning OCR era.

330
00:17:24.877 --> 00:17:28.414
And it's clearly a strong engine that beats traditional OCR

331
00:17:28.814 --> 00:17:30.716
on many of these real-world images.

332
00:17:31.216 --> 00:17:35.587
But it still mostly thinks in terms of individual lines of text.

333
00:17:36.822 --> 00:17:38.824
Then we added the layout detection,

334
00:17:38.924 --> 00:17:41.527
and that gave you some of this region level structure.

335
00:17:41.727 --> 00:17:44.329
So where are the paragraphs, the tables and the figures,

336
00:17:44.630 --> 00:17:47.132
but it's still not full semantic understanding.

337
00:17:47.733 --> 00:17:51.837
And it doesn't really represent the way that humans see documents.

338
00:17:52.538 --> 00:17:55.941
And humans really operate very much on a visual system.

339
00:17:56.542 --> 00:17:59.945
So we'll continue to bring more of these concepts from vision

340
00:18:00.245 --> 00:18:02.147
as we move forward in the course.

341
00:18:02.581 --> 00:18:05.184
In the next lesson, we'll hand it back to David,

342
00:18:05.184 --> 00:18:08.187
and he'll double click on some of these concepts

343
00:18:08.187 --> 00:18:10.389
around layout and reading order

344
00:18:10.389 --> 00:18:14.059
in a lesson called layout detection and reading order.
